 "summary": "Not as a direction signal. With default settings, price did no better than after random entries when a squeeze fired with positive momentum, on 1h, 4h or daily. Momentum crossing up through zero was the same as random too. Two settings beat random: a BB multiplier of 1.5 on 1h and daily, and the long side out of a squeeze with rising momentum on 1h. Both fire in volatile candles in a window where price rose, so price moved more after them; they do not show which way it would move.",
 "verdicts": [
  {
   "use": "Squeeze firing with positive momentum (default settings)",
   "verdict": "none",
   "text": "Same as random on 1h, 4h and daily, in uptrends and in downtrends."
  },
  {
   "use": "Squeeze firing with positive momentum, BB multiplier 1.5",
   "verdict": "edge",
   "text": "Beat random on 1h and daily but not on 4h, and on daily only in uptrends. These entries cluster in volatile candles in a window that rose, so the result comes from when they fire, not from direction."
  },
  {
   "use": "Momentum crossing up through zero",
   "verdict": "none",
   "text": "Same as random on 1h, 4h and daily with the three pairs pooled. SOL on 1h is the one cell that beat random."
  },
  {
   "use": "Staying long out of a squeeze with rising momentum",
   "verdict": "edge",
   "text": "Beat random on 1h only. On 4h and daily it was the same as random. The entries sit in volatile stretches of a window that rose, so price moved more after them; they do not show which way."
  }
 ],
 "notes": [
  "With default settings, a squeeze firing with positive momentum gave 2,863 entries on 1h. Twenty candles later they were 0.03% behind random entries, inside the luck band. On 4h and daily it was also the same as random. It was the same as random in uptrends and in downtrends.",
  "Setting the BB multiplier to 1.5 gave 2,644 entries on 1h, about the same count as the default. Those entries beat random by 0.15% after 20 candles. The result held without its strongest stretch and at 10 candles, but was the same as random at 50 and 100. In uptrends it rested on signals on Jan 2–3, 2021, and in downtrends it was the same as random. On BTC it rested on Mar 12, 2023, during the Silicon Valley Bank collapse, and on ETH it rested on Aug 18–19, 2026.",
  "On daily, the 1.5 multiplier gave 100 entries, and they beat random by 6% after 20 days. Uptrends carried that result at 7%. In downtrends it was the same as random, and BTC and SOL had too few episodes to judge. A squeeze fires when volatility returns, and the window rose overall. Price moved more after these candles, which does not show that the signal picked direction.",
  "Out of a squeeze with rising momentum is a level condition, so it enters on every candle it holds. It gave 54,610 entries on 1h in 1,615 separate moves. It beat random by 0.11% after 20 candles, and also at 10 and 50 candles, but not at 100. It held without its strongest stretch. In downtrends it held at 0.11%. In uptrends it rested on signals between Aug 23 and Sep 7, 2021. On 4h and daily it was the same as random, so this shape belongs to volatile 1h stretches, not to the direction of the release.",
  "Momentum crossing up through zero was the same as random on all three timeframes with the pairs pooled. SOL on 1h beat random by 0.21%, and held without its strongest stretch and at 10 candles. On 4h, crosses in downtrends were 0.46% ahead, but only because of signals between Aug 13 and Aug 17, 2026. ETH on 4h was 0.68% ahead at 20 candles only. In uptrends the 4h cross was the same as random.",
  "For a long DCA bot, the default squeeze firing adds no timing on these pairs, and the take profit and DCA orders do the work. The 1.5 multiplier and out of a squeeze with rising momentum on 1h mark candles after which price moved further. That makes them a filter for when a take profit may be reached sooner, not a call on direction. This breaks in a falling market, because the same bigger moves then run against a long. The zero cross is a setting to leave out unless you trade SOL on 1h."
 ],
